            "text": "{\"claims\":[{\"type\":\"scope\",\"location\":\"(x : Fin n → ℝ)\",\"explanation\":\"The proposition fixes x before choosing N, c, and a. The informal phrase “expressed identically” calls for one choice of N, c, and a that works for every x. This is a scope difference, although both statements may be provable for the specified admissible coefficients.\",\"prediction\":\"With the declaration hypotheses dropped, a separation test should find an admissibility predicate for which the source conclusion holds at a particular x but no fixed N, c, and a work for every x.\",\"probe_kind\":\"separation\",\"comparison\":null,\"witnesses\":\"(n : ℕ) (x : Fin n → ℝ) (avals : ℕ → (ℕ → Fin n → ℝ) → Prop)\",\"edit_from\":\"(∏ i : Fin n, x i) = ∑ i : Fin N, c i * (∑ j : Fin n, a i j * x j) ^ n\",\"edit_to\":\"∀ x : Fin n → ℝ, (∏ i : Fin n, x i) = ∑ i : Fin N, c i * (∑ j : Fin n, a i j * x j) ^ n\",\"bridge\":\"schema\",\"obligation\":\"The separation drops npos and havals and establishes only a difference in logical form. Review whether “expressed identically” requires uniform coefficients; this probe does not show a failure for the benchmark’s admissibility predicate.\"}],\"summary\":\"The testable suspicion is that x is quantified outside, rather than inside, the existential choice of the identity’s coefficients.\"}"
